A||||5||Philphine||thanks for the warning||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||11-15-2001||11:49 AM||||<BR>read an article in the local paper where '96 and newer cars will be vet tested differently. they're working on hooking up to the diagnostic port of newer cars and getting emissions info direct from the on board computer instead of a pipe test. they say it will be quicker and more convienient for new car owners. they hope to have it going by spring.<P>i'm already wishing all my cars were older than '84 so i didn't have to lift the hood (just an exhaust check). they also mentioned future stuff like onstar type emmisions checks were the car is sending data to satellite and you get a notice from out of nowhere to get your car fixed.   <P>don't expect to see me on any new car lots.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||11-15-2001||12:09 PM||||What you are referring to on the 96+ cars is the OBD-II Diagnostics system.  maybe this will add to the convenience of owners who do not want to wait for a tailpipe test, but as the owner of a 97 Probe in an area that does not require emissions testing, I find it simply an added expense.<P>The real difference with the OBD-II systems is that they started placing higher tech Oxygen sensors in the exhaust, and lots of them.  On my 97 Probe, I happen to have two of them but on V6 cars there are three, and I have heard of some that have four!  <P>The real bummer of these sensors... they are a little expensive.  I do not know what they run for all cars, but my front sensor is $299 and the rear is $385!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Simply outrageous in my opinion!  They need to be replaced when they go bad too.  I have talked to other Probe owners who have seen milage drop to around 10 MPG (normally well in the 20's!) when these things die!||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRiAD||11-15-2001||12:23 PM||||Yup, OBDII. <BR>The "onstar" thing you mention is OBDIII.<BR>It will be a box you drive by alongside the road that will prompt your car to make an update report, and a second one that will retrieve it. No more "going to get tested" and no more "my area doesn't test". <P>They can already tell (with OBDII) many things, ancluding a "map" of your throttle position (how often are you floored), your speed (how oftern are you at "X" speed), if/when/how often you've hit your rev limiter and/or speed limiter, etc..<P>OBDII is an inch away from an unconstitutional invasion of privacy. OBDIII crosses the line, IMHO. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">    <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||11-15-2001||06:23 PM||||I'm all for a clean environment, and safe roads.. but it needs to be done by US choosing to drive safely, (heck I often hit 85mph on my way to work, but there are no houses along that road... way out in the country) and as for emissions, the only reason we still have pathetically low mileage like 30mpg, is the government gets so much money from taxes on gasoline. 60-100+mpg was possible back in the 80s. (anyone remember Smokey Yunick's vapor fuel engine?) there is NO reason we can't have clean running cars that get great mileage and still have decent power, without an automotive Big Brother watching us..<BR>and as soon as they start recording your top speed to play back later to a recording station... people will get tickets for pegging their speedo on a deserted road, etc.. that'll be the first sign of death for the sports car.<P>it's not only unconstitutional, that much of a privacy violation is <B>IMMORAL</B>.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000004||Mach10||11-15-2001||06:36 PM||||HAHAHA What a waste of technology. Sorry, but it's mid-november, and there's no snow up here. IF THAT'S global warming, give me an axe and a pile of dead refridgerators.<P>Join me in O/T in 1/2hr for a conversation on this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||theogre||11-15-2001||08:30 PM||||Actually in the places that have inspections, and that list will likely grow, the connection to the PCM is likely to be in addition to the tail pipe and other emmisions tests already done. It's not intended at all as a time saving item. (Delaware is pondering this in their ever failing attempts to meet federal clean air standards.) This is being done to make it harder to gimmic a car thru emmisions. Any stored codes will probably mean instant failure.<P>The radio based reporting, if it ever happens to cars, will be as easy to implement as cameras at intersections. With a couple narrow exceptions no chalenge to those cameras has stood up in court. The cameras are likely here to stay and increase in use.<P>The first thing you all forget is that, unlike guns, you have no constitutional right to operate a motor vehicle. It's a conveinience the state you live in allows you. They can and will regulate it any way they please, especially when large amounts of Federal Highway and other moneys are at stake.<P>Privacy? In a car? Who ever came up with that concept.... The US courts have killed almost all of any privacy you ever had, which was bloody little to start with. Police can search cars with almost nothing for cause. They aren't like houses.... Oh yes they are now that the Fed have their new terrorism rules.... Sealed search warrants have been served all over the place lately. Even the people searched don't know what the cops are looking for.<P>If you drive in any urban area you can't go 10 miles without passing at least one city or state run camera. Many of those cameras are high resoultion, remotely controlled, long range cammeras that can easily see miles. Similar cammeras are now used in many cities to watch everything happening in the viewable range. Many of those cameras are likely to have face recognition software added in the near future.<P>The roads in many areas have embeded sensors in them that monitor vehicle density and speed. These sensors can easily be used with various cameras.<P>City State and Federal DOT regularly records license plate numbers of vehicles entering/leaving/passing thru differant places/things.<P>Nearly all of you buy most of your fuel with plastic, especially when you travel.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
